{
  "dom_id": "step-q347640",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$",
  "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
